## Working principles (Buildee-wide)

### 1. Tickets define the work

Every change you make is driven by a GitHub Issue created by Buildee. The issue
body has a structured task spec. Read it carefully:

- **Goal** — what to accomplish
- **Input context** — Spec/Design version, related files
- **Acceptance criteria** — must all pass for PR to be acceptable
- **Constraints** — design tokens, allowed dependencies, etc.

If the issue is ambiguous, leave a comment with `[STATUS:CLARIFICATION_NEEDED]`
and stop. Do not guess and proceed.

### 2. Status updates via comments

Update the issue with status as you work. Use these prefixes:

- `[STATUS:STARTED]` — work begun, scope confirmed
- `[STATUS:WORKING]` — implementing changes
- `[STATUS:GUARDRAIL_RUNNING]` — running build/lint/domain checks
- `[STATUS:GUARDRAIL_FAILED]` — checks failed; explain which and why
- `[STATUS:READY_FOR_REVIEW]` — PR opened, awaiting user
- `[STATUS:ERROR]` — unrecoverable error; explain

The Buildee server parses these prefixes and notifies the user in real time.

### 3. PR conventions

- Branch name: `ticket-{ticketId}-{short-slug}`
- Commit messages: `[ticket-{id}] <imperative summary>`
- PR title: same as issue title
- PR description: link to issue + summary of changes + screenshots if visual

### 4. Layered changes

Buildee separates layers (Spec → Design → Code). Always change the highest
applicable layer first:

- If user asks for a new section → update Design (Composition), then Code follows
- Don't hardcode values in Code that should live in Content/Config

### 5. Design tokens are mandatory

This project has a generated design system in `src/design/tokens.ts` (or similar).

- Use only these tokens for colors, fonts, spacing, shadows
- Hard-coded hex colors (`#FF0000`, `rgb(...)`) → guardrail rejection
- New tokens require a separate Design System ticket; don't invent them

### 6. Dependencies allowlist

Adding new npm packages requires the package to be in the allowlist
(`buildee-allowed-deps.json` at repo root). Allowed in MVP:

- `react`, `react-dom`, `next`
- `tailwindcss`, `clsx`
- `zod`
- All `@buildee/*` workspace packages

If you need something else, open the ticket comment with
`[STATUS:CLARIFICATION_NEEDED]` and ask. Don't `npm install` outside the list.

### 7. Traceability

Every component and page must have a comment linking back to the source:

```tsx
/**
 * Generated from: SpecVersion sv_xxx, DesignVersion ds_yyy
 * Ticket: #N
 */
export function Hero() { ... }
```

This is what lets users (and you, in the next ticket) ask "why is this here?".

### 8. Guardrails — what will reject your PR

The server runs gate checks before user review:

- **Layer 1 (build)**: `npm run build` must succeed
- **Layer 2 (lint)**: ESLint errors block; warnings OK
- **Layer 5 (domain rules)**:
  - No hardcoded colors/fonts
  - Dependencies match allowlist
  - Traceability comments present in changed files
  - All `[data-editable]` attributes preserved on existing editable elements

If a guardrail fails, fix the cause (don't disable the rule) and update the PR.

### 9. Don't touch

Files you should never modify:

- `.github/workflows/*` — CI managed by Buildee
- `buildee.config.ts` (or similar) — system config
- `CLAUDE.md` (this file) — system gen

If a change requires touching these, leave a comment and stop.

